In article <528@niktow.canisius.edu> pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:
> The ALPHA chip is impressive, as are the proposed implementations of it
> that I have "heard" about.  But there is one "gotcha" in this for some of us 
> in the future. Per DEC's recently-released info (on comp.arch):
>
>   "Alpha includes a very flexible privileged library of software for oper-
>    ating system-specific operations, invoked with PALcalls. This library
>    allows Alpha to run full VMS using one version of this software library
>    that mirrors many of the VAX operating-system features, and to run OSF/1
>    using a different version ....., and sumilarly for NT."
>
>  So (looking for a cloud on a sunny day): what does this do to those who
>  might wish to delve in the used system market ?  On top of the existing
>  VAX<->ULTRIX license hassles for current VAX processors, do we also have
>  to hassle with replacing a PAL chip ?  Will DEC even market same ???  

You've been confused by what might be considered an unfortunate choice
of name.  "PAL" stands for Privileged Architecture Library; it has nothing
to do with the kind of PAL that comes in a little plastic package with
lots of pins.  It's entirely software.  Think of it (if you must) as
"loadable microcode."

PAL code is sort of like locore.s in Unix, except that there
are some instructions that can *only* be used in PAL mode.  PAL provides
an efficient way for applications (and the kernel) to do things that
are privileged without paying the cost of a full system call.  For
example, some of the weirder VAX instructions are emulated using PAL
routines.  The idea is that the PAL entry points are protected (i.e.,
an unprivileged program cannot replace PAL code) and the PAL routines
do any necessary protection checks, so they can safely execute nasty
instructions and yet be callable directly from user code.

Presumably, the boot ROMs on your system will load the right PAL code
somehow.  Not having an Alpha to play with, I don't know quite how
this will be done.  I assume that Digital isn't interested in stocking
two different kinds boot ROMs for each machine, so I wouldn't worry
about having to swap those, either.

-Jeff "not an expert on this (yet)"


