This is an invitation to all present or former DEC-10 (PDP10) buffs to
indulge in some nostalgia. 

We are offering you the chance to: 

1. Try yourself out as a DEC-10 buff, 

2. Send us a greeting to be read at the final wake for our dearly
departing DECsystem-1091, 

3. Send us nostalgia stories about DEC-10s. We will collect these and
make them available to the community. 

4. HAVE YOUR OWN PERSONAL PPN on our system to play with as you wish for
a few weeks to renew your acquaintance with TOPS-10 and to read the
greetings and stories. You will be able to use most of the well known
editors, languages and some games common on DEC10s. 

To accept this invitation telnet to DEC10.QUT.EDU.AU and type LOG
777,7777. The password is the name of the operating system. As part of
the login procedure the dungeon master will test your worthiness as a
DEC10 buff. 

Note that this invitation is ONLY VALID OUTSIDE THE HOURS OF 7 AM TO 9
PM BRISBANE TIME MONDAY TO FRIDAY. Australian EST is 10 hours ahead of
GMT. 

That is the system will be available by Telnet as follows.

           11 am to 9 pm GMT,
            6 am to 4 pm New York time,
            3 am to 1 pm California time,
and from Friday morning to Sunday afternoon in those areas. 

Alternatively, if you are local or telephone costs don't worry you, you
may dial in to (07) 229 6111 or (07) 221 0700. The service required is
SERVER and the reply to the LOCAL prompt is C DEC10. The international
country code for Australia is 61 so dial international 617 instead of
07. Speeds are 110 to 2400 bps but US (Bell) modems may not work. THE
TIME RESTRICTIONS DO NOT APPLY to dial in. 

This KL 1091 with 2048 K words is no longer doing useful work and is no
longer being maintained. If it keeps going there will be a closing
ceremony and wake at a date to be determined probably in mid March. The
invitation ends when the system stops. 

We are looking at the possibility of enabling those logged in at the
time to share in the wake. 

During Australian working hours the Telnet/LAT translators will be
required for more serious purposes and only one channel will be
available to the DEC10. During the hours given above there may be up to
20 channels. 

Bill Fisher 


