>>   a little about these systems and how they compare with the DS3100 and
>>   DS5000. 

>>		  Thanks,

>>			  Benjamin


Benjamin,

  The DS2100 was a cost reduced version of the DS3100. You can find a pretty
complete description of the product in the DS3100 functional specification that
is available for anonymous ftp on   gatekeeper: /pub/DEC/specs  

The DS2100 was announced as a 10.4 MIP system with the DS3100 then being a
14.3 MIP system. I think the SPEC ratings (which I prefer to use where I can)
were ~8.3 and ~11.9 respectively, but it's been a while.  

The main points of difference run something like this for the base systems 
(this is from memory,there are NO guarantees as to accuracy and you'd be much 
better off getting the real documentation from your local sales folks):


		DS2100	  DS3100   DS5000/xx	DS5000/1xx	DS5000/2xx

MHz		  ??  	    16.7    20  25      20  25  33        25   40

SPEC		 ~8.3?	   ~11.9   16.3 19.1   16.4 19.3 25.5   23.5  32.4
     
Memory capacity 24MB	  24MB	     40MB	  128MB		  480MB

TURBOchannel

   slots	No	  No	     2 slots	  3 slots	  3 slots

   clock	na        na         12.5MHz	  12.5MHz	  25MHz


SCSI (1 port)	SCSI-2    SCSI-2     SCSI-2	  SCSI-2	  SCSI-2

Internal disk   yes	  yes	      yes	   yes		   no

Ethernet	Thick/	  Thick/     Thick	  Thick		  Thin (200)
		Thin	  Thin					  Thick(240)

Serial 

   ports	 2	   2	       1            2               2

   type		MMJ       MMJ         25pin	   25pin           25pin

   Synch?	no	  no	      yes	   yes		   no (200)
								   yes(240)

   modem ctl	no	  no	      yes	   yes		   yes


ISDN	        no	  no	      yes	   no		   no

ACCESS.bus	no	  no	      yes	   no		   no

Audio	        no	  no	      yes	   no		   no



NOTE: The ISDN and ACCESS.bus hardware on the Personal DECstation 5000/xx
      machines is real. Software support for both is not yet complete.


  So, if you're looking for a base-level machine with SCSI as it's only
configurability, the DS2100 is not a bad system at all. If you're looking
to be able to migrate to various graphics options or other third party
hardware options, the TURBOchannel based machines are a better choice.

  Sorry if this got a little long winded (I'm actually amazed at how much
of this stuff I THINK I remembered, now if only it's accurate). I hope that
it helps with your question though.

