Gordon,

  Here's the ones I can help with:


>>   Looking for info on the following DEC products considered for purchase:

>>   DEC now offers a 1/4" tape drive - TZK10, which can be used
>>   for backup, etc.  The tapes can hold up to 525Mb.  This is
>>   significantly more than SUN tape drives, but I heard that the
>>   drives are compatible, i.e. you can read SUN tapes on the TZK10 (tar).
>>   Is this true?  

  To my understanding, yes. The DEC QIC (Quarter Inch Cartridge) tape is
  supposed to be able to handle the standard QIC formats.

>>   Also, does anyone know the type of tapes that hold
>>   up to 525Mb and their ballpark price.  I currently have a TK50 and
>>   would like to move to something with more capacity and not too much $$.

  I found the following entry for the tapes on page 175 of DECdirect

		320MB tapes (5/box)	$220
		525MB tapes (5/box)	$375

  although I'm sure you can buy your tapes elsewhere as well.

>>   Will DEC offer software on 1/4 tape soon as well as the CD-ROM and TK50
>>   formats?

  Soon? I certainly hope so. I do know that this is being talked about. I do
  NOT know for sure if it's been decided to do it.

>>   Can one order a DECstation 5000/240 with NO memory?

  I do not know of any oderable part numbers that allow you to buy a system 
  with zero memory. You might try one of the DEC distributors though (like
  Avnet or Wyle) as they might be able to do that for you.

>>   Third party prices
>>   are just too good - just heard $2100 for a 32Mb (4Mb) board of ECC memory.
>>   If one orders a 16Mb system, is it absolutely true that you can use ONE
>>   of the 8Mb boards at the top of memory along with numerous other 32Mb 
>>   boards?

  You have it slightly backwards. The memory stride is set by the first array
  module. You can put in all of your 32MB cards and then put one 8MB card at
  the end of the row and it "should" work (I've never seen it fail on a DS5000)
  all that will happen is that the system believes it has a 32MB card with 24MB
  of bad memory on it.

>>   The DECstation 5000/240 holds no internal drives or tape units. All are
>>   ordered in a SZ12 expansion box.  I was under the impression that one
>>   external unit however - the RRD42-FD CD-ROM drive is in its own box. 
>>   Is this a small box like the SUN CD-ROM drives that can sit on a desktop??

  All of the RRD42 drives I have are embedded in SZ12s. But the picture in the
  DECdirect catalog looks like a small desktop device.

>>   Are all of the 5000/240 systems sold with the HX frame buffer and VRT-16
>>   monitor 72Hz refresh rate and 1280 x 1024 pixels?

  The DS5000/240 can use any of the TURBOchannel graphics options. You can buy
  it with an HX or a TX or a PXG and so on. As for the 1280x1024 resolution
  you mention, that can also be run on a VRT19 (I believe that the VRT19-DA
  SONY monitor is switchable between 66HZ and 72HZ).

>>   Any other impressions on the 5000/240 in general?

  I like 'em a lot ;-)   A lot fewer moving parts than the DS5000/200. And
  some really neat ASIC integration to get rid of all of the programmable
  array logic.

>>   Thanks for any info,
>>   Gordon Jacobs

  Your welcome.

