In article <5479901@MVB.SAIC.COM>, steveg@cseic.saic.com 
(Stephen Harold Goldstein) writes:

> When I try to turn the 8th [async DDCMP] line on, NETACP enters the MUTEX 
> state, and all DECnet operations
> cease for all practical purposes (they go veerrrry slooooowwly).  
> [...]
> Ideally, I'd like to have 31 of my 32 async ports running ddcmp.

Good God.  

> Can anyone provide me with a description of the NETACP$ logicals that
> LOADNET.COM uses when firing up NETACP?  Is there a formula for
> modifying system or process parameters based on the number of asynch
> DECnet lines I want to install, etc.?  Any help would be greatly
> appreciated.

Yah.  before doing @STARTNET, define NETACP$BUFFER_LIMIT to 65535 + (m*n*s),
where m is the number of lines you want (31), n is the number of receive
buffers you're specifying for the async DDCMP lines, and s is the size of the
line buffers (see output of SHOW LINE TX-c-u CHAR).  

Also, increase LRPCOUNT by m*n.  Then increase it by another 25%, or by 30
LRPs, whichever is greater, because I've yet to see a system that wasn't short
on LRPs.  (Not that dynamically expanding the list takes that long.)  I am
assuming you are using some "typical" buffer size, like 576, which will come
out of the LRP list.  See the output of SHOW MEMORY/POOL/FULL for the ranges of
sizes which use the LRP list. 

