>Our lab here uses a VAXstation3200 which has an ADQ32-AA analog-digital 
>converter card in it. This card is quite hip (32 channels, 200kHz peak 
>rate and what not). I've been using it for the past 2.5 years without any 
>problem till last week..... I called Digital Service, and the only idea that 
>they came up with was an exchange price of $4200 (Cdn.) + $ 300 (blood 
>sucking for a 5 minute card slotting job). We had originally purchased the 
>card for $3850.  .......the DC-DC converter on the board and that was 
>definitely not working which means that the board wasn't getting any 
>power. So far so good, we could replace that chip and check the card out.
>I called Digital Service again to find out the specs of the DC-DC 
>converter chip that goes into the ADQ32 board. ...

   This would be a perfect application of the DecMailer Program.  Looking in
my DECmailer catalog, I don't find the ADQ32-AA, but do find the ADV11-A,
A/D for 11/03.  Unless the ident number has changed (very possible), your
probably out of luck for a cheap fix from DEC.  (The Mailer cost for a ADV11-A
is about $ 450).
   This is why DEC sells field service on machines, and buying this card would
have paid for field service for the last couple years.
   DEC field service doesn't desolder 'things' on the boards in the field, so
they wouldn't have a clue what DC-DC converter you need.  If you start 
soldering/desoldering on the board, or hacking it up otherwise, DECmailer
won't accept it either.  If the DC-DC converter fried, you probably have other
components fried as well.  It's the nature of the beast.  If I wanted to
find a replacement DC-DC converter, I'd measure the one I had in every way,
and start with a Newark, Pioneer, or other Electronics supply catalog, and
look over the available units.  Chances are that DEC is using a stock unit
and you can buy it, or adapt a similiar unit for $100 or so.

