In article <1992Feb21.165104.3512@mits.com.au>, richard@mits.com.au writes:
> [omitted]
> 
> Additionally, Why do you reckon the 4000 VLC comes with the thickwire 
> ethernet connector, and not a thinwire. If DEC were listening to customers
> by supporting Asnyc Decnet out of these boxes, surely they've blown it here. 
> (or does everyone except me use thickwire!) It just seems a bit funny.

Well, the VLC is a very cheap and tiny box (VLC stands for
"Very Low Cost", I suppose), so DEC had to save dollars and
size when they put it together.  Having a ThinWire port
costs more & takes up more room than having a ThickWire
port, 'cause a ThinWire board has an on-board transceiver,
while ThickWire uses an outboard transceiver.

IMHO, this is one thing that DEC did right.  If you're only
going to have one flavor of Ethernet, ThickWire is the way
to go.  Think about it: if you have a ThickWire port and
want to attach to a ThinWire cable, all you need is a
little $75 DESTA.  Or if you want to attach to a 10baseT
run, you just need a $75 10baseT MAU.  But if you have only
ThinWire and want to attach to a ThickWire segment, you need
a $600 DESPR (which also increases your repeater count, and
so may kick you into needing to buy a new  bridge - now
you're talking BIG bucks).  Or if you want to attach  a
ThinWire-only station onto a 10baseT run, you'll either need
to  buy a full repeater (see anti-DESPR argument above), or
get one of these nasty non-spec "BNC MAU" gizmos ($350.)

As you can see, a ThinWire-only station is a major bummer unless
you have a ThinWire network already installed.  So going with
a ThickWire-only port is the best compromise.

Aaron

