I'm doing a little footwork on the marketability of an IEEE 802.5 (e.g.
TRN) network interface card for MicroVAX (tm) Q-bus architecture.  We here
have such a beast for our own use, and are wondering if anyone would
be interesting in buying either the design or the boards themselves
(either to use or distribute).

Characteristics of the board include:
    - 802.5 STP DB-9 interface
    - Half-slot Q-bus (MicroVAX II, 3x00/4000 except 3100) form-factor
    - Texas Instruments TMS380C16 TRN chip w/ Texas Instruments
      ring interface parts (no jitter problems)

We have a device driver for VAX Ultrix (tm) versions 3.1 and 4.2.
Since Ultrix's network interface infrastructure is heavily based on
BSD4.3, the device driver should work under pure BSD4.3, but we haven't
proven this yet.  The device driver integrates the board into the
existing network interface infrastructure in the kernel, so TCP/IP,
NFS, etc. work seamlessly (i.e. just as well or poorly as Ethernet :-).
The device driver allows but ignores source routing; all packets
trnsmitted are without source routing fields.

We currently have 5 full-slot prototypes built-up.  They are full-slot
because they have some features we are probably going to eliminate. I
do not know if they may be loaned-out for inspection/evaluation.

We've been using a 4Mb-only board based on the TMS380 for 5 years now.
(Translation: we think we know what we're doing.)

If you are interested in this board, please contact me directly since
I do not regularly follow this newsgroup.

