In article <15460@ncar.ucar.edu>, pack@acd.uucp (Daniel Packman) writes:
|>VMS and unix with their standard shells are not user friendly at all.  The
|>OS that is very user friendly is on a Macintosh and that is why they can
|>still charge more for equivalent or inferior hardware.  Equip VMS or unix
|>with an X windows based shell with pulldowns for all your applications and
|>you can make both rather user friendly.  Equip DOS with windows and you can
|>almost make a silk purse out of a sow's ear.  Who knows?  Maybe next year
|>with Windows Nt one will have a complete silk purse.

Yes, Macs are very user friendly...for the average joe end-user who doesn't
know the difference between big-endian and  little-endian. It is a great
toy, but for most research and development, it is just another proprietary
system that is only as good as the application some company provided for
you to use. Most experienced users (at least as I have read) prefer
a command line interface for speed rather than a GUI and keyboard
combinations can be "taught" to your fingers to gain speed by repetitive
use.

|>The choice between terse and verbose command lines is really a matter of
|>personal preference.  The beginning user usually finds it easier to
|>remember "directory" and "directory/full" rather than "ls" or "ls -l"
|>but most experienced users prefer brief commands and use symbols and
|>logicals in VMS to achieve this.

Again VMS allows a command to be shortened to the smallest amount
of unique characters...try that on UNIX. "dir" can be just as easy as "directory"
and one of the factors, at least in human interfaces, is the learning curve.
Commands like "help" and "directory" are more intuitive than "ls" or "grep"
and command line parameters like "/full"  and "/log" are easier to remember
than "-l" or "-a" and make more sense. (what DOES "grep" mean!  "g"rope for
"r"egular "e"xpressions and "p"atterns?) UNIX users also have a whole slew
of "aliases", similar to the VMS symbols (logicals are equivalent to the
environment variables).

|>In choosing operating systems and hardware one should always examine the
|>application you have in mind and the software that will need to run to get
|>the job done.  If you need to do software development, you will find VMS
|>and unix of various flavors to be quite adequate.  You will find object
|>oriented programming environments available for both.  We have to
|>"accomplish things" and manage to do so with unix and VMS.
|>
|>Dan Packman     NCAR                             INTERNET: pack@ncar.UCAR.EDU

This is always important, especially interms of maintenance, portability, and
available system support for the desired task. We too have the need for both
systems, which is why we are looking to POSIX to help ease some of the problems
mentioned above. Open systems "may" be an oxymoron but it is a step in the right
direction; people are tired of supporting multiple version for various platforms, and
this way the user can choose their OS, and buy the same software...
