In article <1992Feb21.165104.3512@mits.com.au>, richard@mits.com.au writes:
|> Is Async DECNET supported on a 4000 VLC.
|> It wasn't for 3100's, although it worked  (kind of reliably). The options
|> catalog says nothing, except that it has modem control, which kind of implies 
|> to me that it will be supported. 

Yes, the reason that ASync DECnet wasn't supported on the 3100's was because of
a lack of modem control   The VS4000 family includes modem control, so Async
DECnet will be supported 

|> Additionally, Why do you reckon the 4000 VLC comes with the thickwire 
|> ethernet connector, and not a thinwire. If DEC were listening to customers
|> by supporting Asnyc Decnet out of these boxes, surely they've blown it here. 
|> (or does everyone except me use thickwire!) It just seems a bit funny.

Alas, we DID listen, in fact, *I* listened quite a bit regarding the 
modem control on teh Async connector for just the reason above.  I 
personally wrote many of hte market requirements for hte VS4000 family.

As for thickwire, it is the most logical choice when you can have only
one.  The VLC enclosure doesn't have much room for more connections 
to the box, thus we were allowed one connection.  It also turns out that
the thinwire connector is physically bigger in the very critical height
dimension (at least for VLC) than the AUI connector.  


The cost of adapting from thick-to-thin is small compared to thin-to-thick
and adapting from thin-to-twisted pair doesn't exist (that I know of) right 
now.  Adapters from thick-to-twisted pair are available and not terribly
expensive.  (adapters from thick to thin and twisted pair are downright 
cheap from third parties like MiLan and others)

