Prakash,

  You can get a full information packet on ACCESS.bus by contacting either
DEC or Phillips Signetics. You can take the easy way out and just send an
e-mail request to	triadd@pa.dec.com	and ask for an information
kit. I'll try and get the general information posted up to gatekeeper in
the near future (it'll be in   pub/DEC/TriAdd   just like the TURBOchannel
information). We'll get the specifications posted up as soon as they are
released.

  As general information, let me tell you this about ACCESS.bus :

	* It is a joint development between DEC and Phillips Signetics.

	* It is based on the I2C (imagine the 2 as superscript) chip
	  from Phillips. The I-squared-C microprocessor is about a $2.00
	  cost in the US.

	* It is a completely open standard, requiring no license to either
	  system or option vendors.

	* It is a protocol for allowing low speed serial devices (in the
	  75Kbit/s range) to be daisy chained together up to eight meters
	  in length (electrical equivalent) or up to 14 devices.

	* The keyboard/mouse interface on MAXine (sorry, project name, read
	  that as Personal DECstation 5000/xx) is ACCESS.bus in hardware,
	  but until the next version of Ultrix it supports only the DEC mouse
	  and keyboard in software. Just for future reference, the current
	  implementation is referred to as SERIAL.bus on MAXine until we get
	  the expanded support (and don't blame me for the names...).

	* Eventual support will provide generic device drivers that hardware
	  vendors can map to so that their hardware will be supported in an
	  automatic fashion.

	* Examples of some devices that could be used are: mice, keyboards,
	  joysticks, hand held scanners, bar-code readers, etc. .

	* There's a great deal of interest from option vendors over this as
	  it could solve their problem of having to have a separate variant
	  of mouse or keyboard for every major system vendor.

	* Several system vendors are already considering it's use (and no I
	  can't offer any information on who) and we'd be pleased to help
	  them with it.

  I hope that provides a slight view of ACCESS.bus and why we're working on
it. It's one of the five protocols we support out of the TRI/ADD Program.

				Thanx,
				  AJ

