Bill,

  Well, it's actually pretty simple. DEC does have plans for using Futurebus+
on some machines. In fact, I've seen Futurebus+ machines running (Profile B
only). And, for anybody who went to BUScon down in Long Beach California this
month, there was a Futurebus+ Profile B system running there as well. I know
of no current DEC plans to implement Profile A. For those of you who don't
know the profiles, here's a simplistic glimpse:

Profile		Implementation			IEEE Spec Published

  A		System bus			     Yes

  B		I/O Subsystem			     Yes

  D		Desktop profile			     No

  F		Fast System bus			     Yes

  M		Military variant		     Yes

  T		Telecom variant			     No


At least that's my understanding. The machines that I've seen and know of are
all Profile B variations running a 64bit version of Futurebus+. At BUScon the
64bit version of Profile B was demonstrably running 160MB/s. You can get more
information on Futurebus+ from DEC by contacting the TRI/ADD Program.

As for where this all leaves TURBOchannel? TURBOchannel is popping up in more
places than you might imagine (and more places than I can talk about here, and
not necessarily all DEC places).


	VAX			MIPS			ALPHA

	VS4000/60		DS5000/xx		Workstations

				DS5000/1xx
			
				DS5000/2xx

				DS5900

I believe that you'll also find it on the follow-on VAXstation to the VS4000/60
as well as in the follow-on server to the DECsystem 5900. Further product plans
are not at a stage to be disclosed. So, you'll find TURBOchannel up and down
the MIPS product line as well as included in both the VAX and ALPHA products.

For anybody who chose to come to the first TIG (TURBOchannel Industry Group)
session in Long Beach, you would have also heard about the proposals for the
next generations of TURBOchannel. I should also explain at this point that the
TIG is controlling all further changes to the TURBOchannel specifications. DEC
is only one of nine (that's 1 of 9) votes on the steering committee. You can
find out more about TIG and/or join it by contacting Digital's TRI/ADD Program.

The current favorite for the next generation TURBOchannel is a speed selectable
version. Since TURBOchannel is a radial interconnect you can implement it in a
fashion like this:

	1. The system powers up at 25MHz (which matches the current specs).

	2. Upon reading the Option ROM Header file, the system finds that the
	   TURBOchannel Option in Slot 0 is capable of supporting 50MHz.

	3. The system then selectively turns up the clock rate of the Slot 0
	   TURBOchannel allowing that option to run as fast as 200MB/s.

	4. Any current TURBOchannel Options in Slot 1 or Slot 2 continue to
	   function just fine at 25MHz.

The above scenario provides complete backwards compatibility for all existing
TURBOchannel Options, while allowing options which require the high performance
version to be able to use it.

As for where TURBOchannel will be used? Anywhere that a high performance, low
cost, simple interconnect is needed. At 12 control signals (44 signal pins
total) designing to TURBOchannel is hardly rocket science. On the DS5000/2xx
systems you can acheive DMA rates in the high 80's MB/s including software
overhead. Programmed I/O implementations have been done by 3rd parries with as
little as a single 22V10 PROM and complete DMA versions have been done with as
few as 4 22V10 PROMS. The TCI (TURBOchannel Interface) ASIC is expected to be
in the ~$30.00 price range for an ASIC that is capable of 30+ MB/s. Or, in a
ceramic package up to the 80-90 MB/s range. For anybody who'se interested the
current TCI spec is available on gatekeeper.

The RealTime market is moving on TURBOchannel (chomping at the bit for the TCI
ASIC, I might add) because it's a very low cost, low profile, high speed bus.
It's also very inexpensive to design to. At 44 signal pins for 100MB/s you can
compare it to EISA at 153 signal pins running 33MB/s or to 6U VME at 117 pins
running 40MB/s (all of the numbers are architectural). Looking at component
costs (and remembering that pins cost money) you can see why they're interested
in it.

The Multi-Media market has begun to catch on to the fact that cheap bandwidth
is the name of the game. Trying to do high speed networks (like ATMs) or video
teleconferencing (like DECspin) on low speed buses just doesn't hack it. You
can work out the numbers for yourself on sustained data rates and see why. In
addition to the low speeds of AT/ISA/EISA/VME and so on, you have to also work
in the overhead costs of arbitration on the bus. Especially when somebody uses
multiple DMA options on the bus. 


There are certainly things that I WOULDN'T do with TURBOchannel. Like trying
to put 15 or 20 option cards on a single TURBOchannel for a card cage, which
would break the electrical characteristics. But, you could get the same volume
of options out of multiple TURBOchannels in the card cage and still keep your
cost low in comparison with something like VME.

I also wouldn't attempt to use TURBOchannel as the system bus in a true multi-
processor SMP machine. But it would still make a dandy I/O subsystem for such
a machine (hint, can we think of anybody who might want to do that?). After
all, at some point you need to get interesting data into the system memory.
And given the increases in processor performance....well, you wouldn't put a
lawn mower carburetor on a Ferrari engine, would you?

In addition to the 28 shippable TURBOchannel Options that I find in the TRI/ADD
Shippable Products catalog and the 13 TURBOchannel Options listed in the Coming
Attractions section (which means the hardware is ready and the software is
under-way) let me tell you about some other interesting developments:

	* An HDTV Frame Buffer (I've seen the hardware and proto X-server
			        running).

	* A 2K x 2K graphics controller.

	* An SCI (Scalable Coherent Interface) option. High speed network.

	* A HIPPI network option.

among others.


Anyway, this is all probably more than you ever really wanted back in answer
to your original post. But, I've been away from the net so long that I just
got carried away. Sorry.

