|>milliseconds the host sends a 60 byte packet which the server
|>immediately replies to.  One byte in the host
|>packet counts up.  The rest of the bytes never change.  Same
|>thing for the packts coming out of the server.
|>
|>Is this a well known problem?
|>

Bill,

I don't think that this would be a LAT problem.  The problem maybe with your ethernet card, if it is a DEQNA.  The DEQNA can get in a wacky mode via power glitches where it will start doing strange LAT and DECNET things.  eg.  send out LAT stop messages for circuits that exist between 2 different machines.

These problems were fixed in the DELQA ( 2nd generation Enet controller ).

\kg


