rate and what not). I've been using it for the past 2.5 years without any 
problem till last week. For no apparent reason (at least nobody input any 
nasty voltages into it) the card seems to have conked out and the control 
software reads all channels at a flat 10 volts (the peak value) even 
though there are no inputs. The curious part is that two separate pieces 
of software read the same values and the software does not abort after 
flagging any errors i.e. the programs seem to think that the card is OK.

Now comes the nasty part. I called Digital Service, and the only idea that 
they came up with was an exchange price of $4200 (Cdn.) + $ 300 (blood 
sucking for a 5 minute card slotting job). We had originally purchased the 
card for $3850.  Another company in Vancouver that deals in and services 
Digital products said that though they couldn't repair it, they had 
contacts in the US or Canada who could. However, they called back later 
and said that nobody was willing to touch that card.

I called DEC Direct to get a hold of the Print-Set (circuit schematics 
etc.) for the card so that one of our techs could try to isolate the 
problem, but Digital doesn't seem to have any around (is this a 
professional organization or a bunch of clowns ?).

I was desperate, and so we pulled the board out of the machine anyway. On 
a hunch, we checked the DC-DC converter on the board and that was 
definitely not working which means that the board wasn't getting any 
power. So far so good, we could replace that chip and check the card out.
I called Digital Service again to find out the specs of the DC-DC 
converter chip that goes into the ADQ32 board. They are yet to call me 
back. 

I'll be extremely thankful if anyone out there who has any info re. my 
problem (esp. if they know of a suitable replacement DC-DC 5 to +/- 15 
volt converter chip we can buy for the ADQ32-AA board). Any phone #s of 
