In article <1992Feb20.024711.25382@cs.ubc.ca>, Sundar_Prasad@mtsg.ubc.ca (Sundar) writes:
> Now comes the nasty part. I called Digital Service, and the only idea that 
> they came up with was an exchange price of $4200 (Cdn.) + $ 300 (blood 
> sucking for a 5 minute card slotting job). We had originally purchased the 
> card for $3850.  Another company in Vancouver that deals in and services 
> Digital products said that though they couldn't repair it, they had 
> contacts in the US or Canada who could. However, they called back later 
> and said that nobody was willing to touch that card.

  Well, Field Service is just that - service in the field. If you don't have
a contract, they charge you the board swap plus the service call. That isn't
the only option you have from DEC, though.

  In the US, a program called "Digital Assisted Services" makes parts and
various repair programs available at substantially lower prices. "Do it your-
self and save", sort-of. They're at 800-225-5385, although I don't know if
that number will work from Canada or not. Two services they offer are "DEC-
mailer" and "Factory Repair".

> I called DEC Direct to get a hold of the Print-Set (circuit schematics 
> etc.) for the card so that one of our techs could try to isolate the 
> problem, but Digital doesn't seem to have any around (is this a 
> professional organization or a bunch of clowns ?).

  DECdirect works by you giving them a part number, then they give you the
price and lead time. Do you have the exact part number for the print set, or
did you just ask them for it by name?

  In the US, diaing DECdirect gets you a voice menu offering you the chance
to A) buy something if you know the part number, or B) receive technical
pre-sales assistance. If your DECdirect doesn't do that, then you could by-
pass the whole thing by calling Part Number Assistance at 603-884-5000. Just
tell them you want the part number for the Maintenance Print Set for your
board.

> I called Digital Service again to find out the specs of the DC-DC 
> converter chip that goes into the ADQ32 board. They are yet to call me 
> back. 

  Which piece of Digital did you talk to? Again, the only folks who would
know this info are the Part Number Assistance people (number above). Once
you get the part number, they will probably tell you it is an "Unlisted
Part" which means it isn't normally available through DECdirect. However,
Unlisted Parts will accept an order for it in almost all cases.

  In closing, I think that DEC probably did a bad job of explaining to you
how to go about getting this module fixed, but I *don't* think you're being
"screwed by DEC".

