  The answer is primarily at your end, for you to determine, based on how
  you are using the machine: the 3100  will do fine until it "runs out of
  memory" and begins using the disk as a substitute.  Run vmstatto see whether
  this is occurring; once it starts, performance will drop off very badly,
  obviously.

  In our case, we have our 3100s maxed out to their 24 MB limit and that is
  still not enough: we use them for database software development and we have
  a copy of our commercial dbms running on all of them for testing.  The dbms
  uses a lot of memory, the compilations use a lot of memory, and the systems
  begin to swap when two programmers are both compiling.  Your Mileage May 
  Vary depending on what you are doing.

> I'm a Sun man, myself, but I can't believe that the DS3100 is _that_
> bad -:)  

  Several years ago, we ran benchmarks - based on OUR application mix, using
  "examples" of our applications.  Most of these would thus not have much 
  meaning to you.  But one may be somewhat apropos, tho here we are mixing 
  apples (software differences) and oranges (hardware differences):  compilat-
  ions of C host language database applications. Time is average total elapsed
  per process (e.g., total elapsed time for all processes / no. of concurrent
  processes):

    system    MHz  Memory    1 process    4 concurrent    8 concurrent 

    SUN 4/370 25    32          126            339             728

    DEC 3100  17    12          181            543            1035

  Note that the above were SYSTEMS, not workstations, e.g., no X-window 
  processes to chew up memory.  Other benchmarks, by the way, showed
  roughly similar results, tho in some cases, the SUN "beat" the 3100
  by a wider margin at the 1 or 4 process runs.

> their perfectly good grant money to buy a Sun, because the DEC is so
