  By "vax 5500 and 5900s" I assume you mean DECsystem 5500 and DECsystem 5900.
  The reason for pointing out this distinction isn't simply to reinforce mar-
  keting nomenclature: "vax" systems are built around vax cpus and run either
  VMS or ULTRIX, while "DECsystems" are built around MIPS Corp cpus and run 
  only ULTRIX.

  The 5500 and 5900 are perfectly good machines.  The primary system of that 
  genre that was "in trouble" was the multiprocessor 58nn series, whose perfor-
  mance fell "somewhat" short of claims (note that this wasn't intentional
  deception on DEC's part: it assumed performance based on the capability of
  the cpu it put in these machines, but the sum total of the parts was less
  than the individual parts themselves).  Recent correspondence indicates that
  the machine has been improved somewhat.

  All of the above systems differ from the DEC 5000 series in one major respect
  that may be important to you:  they contain the same cpus as the 5000s - 
  both series have cpus that vary in clock rate, but otherwise they are all
  comparable - but they come in relatively large boxes with relatively high
  price tags.  One "advantage" is that you can put lots of disks in them and
  thus keep things tidy.  Another is that they also include proprietary DEC
  i/o interfaces - the DSSI interconnect - this is an "advantage" if you 
  see the DSSI as an "advantage" over SCSI.  But you can construct a comparable
  system at much lower cost by purchasing a DEC 5000 series machine, throw in
  an extra SCSI interface or two, and hang external SCSI disks on it.  You may
  also want to investigate the potential utility, to your application, of an 
  add-on Prestoserve i/o accelerator: these come standard on the big boxes
  but are an option on the 5000s.


