In article <1992Feb14.195117.14661@telebit.com>, eric@telebit.com (Eric Smith) writes:
> Does the RQDXn have the cylinders, heads, etc. pre-programmed in its
> firmware?  Is it possible to hack the firmware to recognize different
> drives?

  I see an archived posting of mine has been sent out, which should answer
all of your earlier questions. I'll give some mre details on these last 3.

  The RQDX1 and 2 have the drive parameters in ROM, and prod the drive in
various unusual ways to try to figure out what type it is. Not only do you
need identical geometry, you need a drive that reacts to illegal commands
the exact same way. The 1 and 2 also write the firmware revision of the
controller to the drive, and that drive will not be usable on a controller
with earlier firmware unless it's reformatted.

  The RQDX3 has a table of pre-defined drives in ROM, but when it formats
a drive it writes the parameters to the disk (either one of the pre-defined
sets or user-supplied sets). Note that doing user-supplied parameters is
really hairy (you can construct something that looks good, but will wreck
the format when a bad block is detected).
 
> And while we're on the subject, is there a document that I can get from
> DEC that gives the specifications of MSCP (and maybe TMSCP)?

  You can buy the MSCP Architecture documents from DEC, either alone (ex-
cept for the license) or as part of a complete training course. Even the
cheap way out is a few thousand dollars, though. (At least it was when I
looked into it).

> If anyone has an RQDX3 for sale, I'm interested.

  DEC was selling refurbed ones (B2-RQDX3) for $300, last time I checked.
They may be out of them by now. Computer Clearing House in Rochester, NY
(area code 716, sorry I don't have the full number at home) advertises 'em
for somewhere between $150-$200. I've dealt with them and they are a lot
better than you'd guess from their little add and low prices.

