In article <1992Feb4.183852.9698@news.cs.indiana.edu>, anlhille@arapahoe.ucs.indiana.edu (Guess who?) writes:
> Can someone please mail me a list of DEC's line of MIPS-based
> workstations, current and up-and-coming, including stats such as which
> processor, hard drive/ram space, price (retail and educational), and
> anything else you can drag up?

    In rough cronological order:

	DECstation 3100 - The orignal, an R2000A.  I don't recall the
			  clock speed.  Max memory 24 MB using MS01
			  parity SIMMs.  Your choice of SCSI disk from
			  the RZ23 to the RZ58.  Your choice of a single
			  plane monochrome frame buffer to an 8 plane
			  color frame buffer.  LANCE based Ethernet adapter
			  on the system board.  Your choice of thinwire or
			  thick.  Approximate speed: 12 Specmarks.

			  Current starting price: $5000.

	DECsystem 3100S - The above without either frame buffer.  Intended
			  as a server system.  Eventually the 'S' was dropped.

	DECstation 2100 - A slower, lower cost version of the DECstation 3100.
			  Approximate speed: 8 Specmarks.

			  Current starting price: $5000.

	DECsystem 5400  - Office friendly, deskside server system.  It used
			  the R3000 and a relativly slow memory subsystem.
			  It may have used MicroVAX 3800/3900 family memory
			  with a max of either 64 MB or 128 MB.  It could
			  support up to two Q-bus disk controllers, which
			  at the time were either the KFQSA (DSSI disks) or 
			  the KDB50 (SDI disks).  With RA92s max disk space
			  available is 12 GB.  With RF73s it's 14 GB.  Speed
			  will be in the 15 Specmark range, but I don't recall.

			  Current price: ~$19,000

	DECsystem 5810  - An R3000 based system built on the same platform as
	DECsystem 5820    the VAX 6000 family.  This gave the advantage that
	DECsystem 5830    you could use many of the same peripherals as the
	DECsystem 5840    VAX 6000s.  It suffered from relatively poor memory 
			  performance and a bad rep.  Never the less it was
			  still a 10 Specmark system, which was faster than
			  VAX systems available at the time.  Max memory 
			  capacity is 256 MB and max supported disk space 
			  capacity is 144 GB.  Unsupported max disk configur-
			  ation would push that to ~380 GB.  Each CPU was a
			  pair of boards, one for the R3000 and the other a
			  VAX to run self tests and diagnostics.  This kept
			  us from having to rewrite all the diagnostics.

			  The obvious disadvantage of this configuration is
			  a four CPU system used 8 XMI backplane slots, leaving
			  only five for memory and one for the VAXBI.  Or four
			  and two VAXBIs.

			  Starting price: $54,000

	DECsystem 5500

		The follow-on of the DECsystem 5500.  A memory subsystem
		designed to go with the CPU give it the speed that the
		5400 lacked.  It has a max memory capacity of 256 MB.
		The I/O subsystem includes a SCSI adapter, DSSI adapter,
		2nd generation Ethernet controller (SGEC) on the I/O and
		Q-bus adapter on the I/O module.  The system includes a 
		Prestoserve tightly bound with the CPU.  The KDA50 SDI
		disk controller, KFQSA DSSI adapter and KZQSA SCSI adapter
		are supported disk expansion off the Q-bus.

		Starting price around $30,000.

	DECsystem 5100

		Small office server system.  Prestoserve was optional and
		I'd guess it used DECstation 3100 memory but I'm not sure.
		Expansion capacity was limited to the one SCSI adapter.
		It used some flavor of the R3000.  There is space in the
		system box for 3 1/2" SCSI disks.

		Starting price around $9,000.

	DECstation 5000/200

		The first R3000 based DECstation.  Max memory is 480 MB.
		Max disk space with the built-in SCSI adapter and two
		TURBOchannel SCSI adapters is 27 GB, this with 21 RZ58s.
		(No tapes though).  A wide range of TURBOchannel graphics
		options is available from framebuffers upto hardware assisted 
		3D.  The TURBOchannel is a 100 MB/sec bus, with specifications
		available via ftp from gatekeeper.dec.com.  There's also a
		support group available for 3rd party vendors wishing to build
		TURBOchannel things.  Other TURBOchannel things avaialble from
		DEC are an Ethernet adapter and two flavors of FDDI adapters.

		Current starting price: $9,000

	DECsystem 5000/200

		The above with no graphics.  This opens up a TURBOchannel
		slot.

	DECstation 5000/{120,125,133}

		A cost reduced R3000 based system.  It uses the MS01 memory
		used on the DECstation 3100, with the option of using the
		higher density MS01-CAs.  The TURBOchannel is a slowed down
		50 MB/sec version.  The CPU is implimented on daughter card
		that currently comes in three versions; a 20 MHz, 25 MHz
		and 33 MHz version.  The Model 120 starts at around $9,000.
		Speeds vary according to the daughter card.

	DECstation 5000/240

		The follow-on of the DECstation 5000/200.  This one uses a
		40 MHz R3000A.  Same memory and disk capacity as the Model
		200.  Full speed TURBOchannel with the CPU on a daughter
		card.  Speed I think is up around 32 specmarks.  All the
		TURBOchannel options should be supported, though the newer
		ones are more interesting than the older ones.
	
		Starting price around $11,000.

	DECsystem 5000/240

		No graphics, server system.  A Prestoserve license is
		available.

	DECstation 5000/{20,25}

		The Personal DECstation.  Another TURBOchannel based
		system.  CPU daughter cards come in 20 MHz and 25 MHz
		flavors.  Max memory is 40 MB.  The system has built-in
		audio and color support.  There's also space internally
		for a 3.5" SCSI drive and 3.5" floppy.

		Prices start around $4,300.  Perfornance in the 16 to
		19 Specmark range.

	DECsystem 5000/25

		A server version of the Model 25 above.

	DECsystem 5900

		Using the same CPU board as the DECsystem 5000/240 this
		system includes the Prestoserve license and hardware and
		comes in a cabinet designed to allow easy expansion up
		to 28 SCSI disks and tape.  Buried in some of the marketing
		information is mention of a CI adapter planned for the
		system allowing the system access to HSC based SDI disks.
		See the 5800 description for the current maximum supported
		disk space configuration.  Maximum memory configuration is
		448 MB (one slot is needed for the Prestoserve).  It has the
		same performance as the Model 240.  Prices start at around
		$59,000.

	Future stuff?

		The announcements of these systems included program 
		announcements to build R4000 daughter cards for some
		of these systems.
		
-- 
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com



