the differences between the SCSI controllers have no bearing on your problem.
The Drive shelf is different for those two part numbers.  One shelf allows you
to physically mount 2 3 1/2 drives and one 5 1/4 half height drive.  The other
shelf only allows you to mount 1 3 1/2 drive and one 5 1/4 half height drive.

fyi:  there are 2 main variant SCSI boards that you will find.  Each of
these comes in two flavors.  There's the LARGE board that takes up
the entire width of the drive shelf and then there's the SMALL board
that takes up half the width of the drive shelf.  The two flavors are: 2 SCSI
controllers on the board, or 1 SCSI controller and 1 Floppy disk controller on
the board.

Just for yuks,

here's a map of the connectors on the small SCSI controller.

                                     (A)
                                  +=========+
                     +-----------------------+
                     |  ++  +=======+ (D)    |
                     |  ||(B)                |
                     |  ||                   |
                     |  ||                   |
                     |  ||                   |
                     |  ||           (C)     |
                     |  ++        +========+ |
                     +-----------------------+

(A) is the 68 Pin External SCSI Connector (EXTERNAL Bus, DKBxxx)
(B) is an internal SCSI Connector (INTERNAL Bus, DKAxxx)
    usually has 2 or 3 50 pin SCSI connectors on the attached cable.
(C) is an internal SCSI Connector (EXTERNAL Bus, DKBxxx) or a floppy 
    drive connector (actually there are two if its a floppy.)
(D) is a connector for a ribbon cable that connects the SCSI controller to the 
    system's motherboard.

