Organization: Rochester Institute of Technology
Date: Mon, 10 Feb 92 16:38:12 GMT

Howdy...  We are currently involved with a project to get
an X11 server running on some unique hardware we've got here.
The problem is, we can't seem to find *any* sort of technical
documentation for these workstations.  The workstations are
packaged as Kodak KIMS (Kodak Image Management System) workstations,
but they were built by DEC (presumably for Kodak).

The hardware is as follows:  the system board is basically a
VAXstation(or MicroVAX)-2000 system board, without the hard disk
controller, the SCSI tape-drive controller and the "base"
monochrome video hardware (see below for the graphics hardware).
The rest of the system *is* a VAXstation-2000; although the
system board layout is different, the components are the same,
and it looks like the space for the missing devices (hard
disk controller, etc) *is* on the board, but the components
aren't there.  Attached to the system board is a monster called
the Image-II Video System, or the Actor Video Sub-System (AVSS);
both names are used to describe the same hardware.  This
is the video board, and it's actually more impressive that
the system board.  This board looks like it's similar to
other DEC graphics systems; it has the Adder(DC323) and Viper(DC502)
chips, the "Sprite" cursor chip(DC503), and some rather intense-looking
specialized hardware(including something called a DC534).  Attached
to the video board is the Actor Video Daughter card, which looks
like it's just another plane of video memory with another Viper
chip.  Here are some part numbers from the boards:

	Kodak KIMS workstation:
		- Also known as the VID10-A workstation (to DEC)

		- System board - 5417025 - "Actor System Module"
		- Video board  - 5416975 - "Image-II Video Module"
			       - 5417102 - "Actor Video Daughter Module"
		- Power supply - 5416991 - H-7280-C

	The system also comes with a standard LK-201 keyboard, a VS-XXX
	mouse, and a VR-419 video monitor.

According to the owner's manual for the system, it was designed to
run under VMS (the VMS device driver is called VKDRIVER.EXE) using
some specialized software applications to take advantage of the
heavy-duty video system.

Neither DEC nor Kodak seems to have any idea of where to look for
technical specs, so I am appealing to the Internet community.  :-)
We need technical information describing the graphics subsystem;
we are trying to write a device driver and port the MIT sample
X-server to these systems, and it's mightly slow going without specs.

If anyone has information regarding these systems, or knows of
where I may *find* such information, we would be greatly
indebted...  :-)  Thanks!

