Subject: Infoserver 150 ramblings (useful!)
Message-ID: <1992Feb11.083743.517@fps.mcw.edu>
Date: 11 Feb 92 08:37:43 CST
Organization: Medical College of Wisconsin,  Milwaukee, WI
Lines: 94

In response to a question I posted here a while back (regarding VXT2000
terminals, and how many does an Infoserver 150 support...), I then got
a call from one of the engineers on the INFOSERVER software team.
(rather nice, I thought).   I had a lengthly conversation with
him, and the following are RANDOM observations I learned about the
new infoservers........

1) TWO infoserver 150's.    Beware.  The INFOSERVER 150 comes in TWO
models.  The Infoserver 150 (comes with standard V2.0 software, a
RRD42, and a RZ23L disk.)   Version 2.0 of the software only provides
the "hooks" for VXT terminals.    The standard infoserver 150 is usually
sold with the "disk access" module (software), that allows any client
to access disk services (be it CDROM, or whatever).   TAPE access is
extra.   

The other model is an Infoserver 150/VXT, designed to manage VXT2000
terminals.  This also comes with the standard V2.0 software, but
the hard disk is an RZ24 (twice as big as the RZ23l).  DISK access module
is not usually provided, but can be purchased separately.

2) VXT2000 terminal support.     
*ANY* infoserver (even the infoserver 100) can support VXT2000 terminals
as long as the V2.0 software is loaded, and the VXT software is added.
The infoserver team "guarantees" a minimum of 20 VXT2000 terminals supported
by an infoserver 150/VXT.    The number of terminals supported is based
on what applications get used, how much memory the VXT2000 terminals
have installed, and how much disk space on the infoserver exists.  Any
infoserver can support MORE VXT2000 terminals, simply by adding more SCSI
disk.     Internally, they have approximately 50-60 VXT2000 terminals
(each with 6 or 8MB installed) hanging off of ONE infoserver 150/VXT.

3) MOP images:
One new command, "UPDATE MOP" was designed to update the infoserver
hard disk with MOP images from a special CD.  This CD was never
finished, and may never be.  (a lot of internal battling caused it
to not get done.)     

One nifty feature of version 2.0 is MOP image support.  It is quite
easy to "install" a MOP image (say, for example, the load image for
DECservers) onto a MOP-enabled partition on the infoserver.  As far
as MOP requests go, the infoserver is damned fast.  The infoserver
can support up to *100* simultaneous MOP downline load requests.
(it does this by caching the requests.  unlike VAXen, the infoserver
does not have to "create a process" for each MOP request).

4) VXT2000 load images
VXT2000 terminals *must* get their software from SOMEWHERE.   By
default, it looks for the load image on a Infoserver, but you can
also get load software for VMS (and possibly ultrix?) as well.   
The infoserver software is usually referred to as the "server version"
and the vax software as a "host-version".   Terminals wishing to use
the "host-version" of the software must be configured physically
with a MINIMUM of 10MB of memory.   [probably due to the fact that
without the Infoserver there, the terminal cannot do dynamic paging].

5) more new commands:
Infoserver 2.0 software provides tons of commands to support VXT
stuff, like creating specially-formatted VXT-terminal disk partitions,
etc etc.    This is *only* provided as a means of recovery in case
someone accidently wipes out part of a partition or something.  In
the usual case, the installation of the VXT software takes care of
creating all the right stuff.

6) RRD42 audio port
For those who have seen the standard RRD42 CD drive, you'll notice that
there is an audio port on there as well!   For those who have RRD42
disk drives on a VAXstation, there is a program in  DECW$EXAMPLES
(DECW$CD.C or some such), that allows access to the audio channels
of the disk drive.   Basically, in the RRD42, (like mode other CD
drives), there are TWO "circuits".   a DATA circuit, and a AUDIO
circuit.    The audio circuit can only be accessed by IO$DIAGNOSE calls.
RRD42 drives in vaxstations (i.e., directly attached via SCSI) can access
the audio channel.

However, currently, the network protocols to access the infoserver CD
drives (i.e., LAST etc), do not support the IO$DIAGNOSE calls.  Thus,
currently, you *cannot* access the infoserver CD audio ports.
(however, believe it or not, that *is* on the infoserver "wish list".
It will ONLY happen if there if found a better/more valid reason
to include IO$DIAGNOSE calls in LAST in the future for other reasons....)
--------------------------------------------------

Like I said, misc. ramblings.   Hope this helps some of you guys
better understand the new infoserver capabilities.
(overall, I have been quite impressed by the infoserver capabilities.
No large site should be wishout one!)
