In article <1992Feb12.143409.14141@europa.asd.contel.com>, mclemore@gnd1.wtp.contel.com (KENNY MCLEMORE) writes:
> nfs questions,
>
> i interested in the memory overhead required to support file systems
> mounted nfs, for both client and severs.  
> 
> say you have a 1k file system mounted nfs versus 1gig, is more memory
> required ?

	The memory requirements of NFS support come in form of;
	
	o  the base kernel support
	o  the group of processes that let NFS work

	For the first I built a copy of my kernel to get a baseline.
	Then I built a copy of it with NFS and RPC taken out.  The
	difference in size was about 240 KB.  There may tables that
	are allocated at boot which would use more space, but that's
	a little harder to test.

	The second is a little easier.  I just happen to have two
	systems one setup as a client using the defaults and the
	other as a server with also with the defaults.  On the
	client I have (output of ps aux):

USER       PID %CPU %MEM   SZ  RSS TT STAT   TIME COMMAND
root        66  0.0  0.1   32   16 ?  I      0:00 /etc/biod 4 (four of these)
root        57  0.0  0.2  112   96 ?  I      0:00 /etc/portmap

	This totals 240 KB.  On the server I have:

root       988  0.0  0.7  264  236 ?  I      0:00 /etc/mountd -i
root        60  0.0  0.4  136  112 ?  I      0:00 /etc/portmap
root        69  0.0  0.1   32   16 ?  I      0:06 /etc/biod 4 (4 of these)
root       993  0.0  0.2  140   64 ?  I      2:05 /etc/nfsd 4 (4 of these)

	This totals 1,088 KB.  For the sizes I'm using the SZ field.

	Are there any hidden memory costs?  Does it require memory
	that could be used somewhere else to mount an NFS served
	file system?  I doubt it.  The size of the kernel is pretty
	well fixed once booted.  The mount table is big enough for
	255 file systems.  But you'll that many whether or not you
	use NFS.

	There is big, somewhat hidden cost, but you won't be able to
	get rid of it.  The ability to support more than one file
	system caused the addition of an extra layer and some amount
	of attendent support code; the generic file system.  If you
	could take this out, lots of things would get smaller.

> say you have a 1k file system mounted nfs versus 1gig, is more memory
> required ?

	I don't believe that NFS cares how big the file system is.
> 
> if not required, what are the tradeoffs (ie. you can run with
> 16m of memory and still support a 1gig nfs file system but, but WHAT) ?

	See the previous.
> 
> how about nfs file system usage, 1gig rarely used versus very active 1k ?

	This is where NFS tuning into play.  If you have a busy file
	system (size doesn't matter), you'll probably want more nfsd
	and biod processes.  As these do require more memory, there
	will be a memory cost for more efficient usage.  More of these
	processes are NECESSARY, but they should improve client
	performance.  There's a NFS guide in the ULTRIX doc set that
	should explain what they do.  In the V4.2A doc set is a Performance
	Management guide that should also help.
> 
> are there system tuning parameters normally adjusted to meet
> specific nfs requirements ?

	This isn't particular to NFS, but if you have a read
	intensive environment, more buffer cache may help.  If
	you don't have a system that supports Prestoserve, about
	also you can do to help writes is spread them out over
	more disks.
> 
> thanks in advance for any info, ken
-- 
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com


