In article <00955990.CFB07B20@EA.USL.EDU>, johnson@EA.USL.EDU writes:
> Greetings;
> 
> I recently installed VMS 5.5 on my cluster.  Since then, though, I have had no
> DECnet usage.  It acts as if the license is not installed in that all I can do
> is to "set host 0".  But, when I run the license facility everything seems to
> be hunky-dorey.

  Check to make sure you don't have a DVNETEND or DVNETRTG TSP or SUP enabled.
(TSP's and SUP's are various temporary forms of PAK). You can do a SHOW LICENSE
to see what's out there. For example:

2_SPCVXA::$ sh lic dvnetrtg

Active licenses on node SPCVXA:

DVNETRTG
	Producer: DEC
	Units: 0
	Version: 0.0
	Date:  1-DEC-1990
	Termination Date: (none)
	Availability: E (System Integrated Products)
	Activity: 0  
	MOD_UNITS

  Notice the Date:  1-DEC-1990 field. This means that one or more DECnet 
licenses in the LMF database is limited. In this case, it's the DECnet SUP
included with the VMS V5 update.

  When LMF combines licenses in memory, it adopts the most restrictive terms
of each. You need to do (just once):

$ LICENSE UNLOAD DVNETRTG
$ LICENSE LIST DVNETRTG/FULL

  (you should have two or more of them. One starts AWS-PK and one starts with
something else. We want to disable the AWS-PK one, which is the DECnet SUP).

$ LICENSE DISABLE DVNETRTG/AUTH=AWS-PK-rest-of-number
$ LICENSE LOAD DVNETRTG

> I can't call DEC because as a university I am under CSLG licensing, which lets
> me use the software, if I can find it.  However I don't have the priviledge
> to make any calls to them if I have a problem.

  The CSLG program has a requirement that there be a responsible person at the
school for handling exactly this class of problem. You should check with the
person who gave you the CSLG license tape or file to see who this is for your
school.

