From: Steve Couturie <lhdsy1!fred.lahabra.chevron.com!shc@uunet.UU.NET>

> I have been given the interesting task at work to find out different
> methodologies used for the charging of workstation support. So, if
> you are working for a "support" organization that deals with workstations,
> I would be interested in hearing from your.
> 
*** I work for the Computer support group here at Chevron's upstream
    research lab.   We are in the process of considering modifying 
    our organization, but currently we have 5 people whose charter is
    Unix workstation care and feeding, with the usual additional tasks
    of user support, etc., plus some significant amount of time spent 
    on corporate computer organization/support/standards/etc. issues,
    through meetings, study teams, and the like.  Busy, busy...

    Plus, there are other folks here doing support for our MVS mainframe,
    Cray, VAX/VMS systems, *many* PCs and Macs, the network (up to 
    the workstation ports), plotters, etc.  Oh, and *lots* of tape.

    The local environment has Unix workstations from DEC, Sun, HP (and 
    old Apollos), SGI, Stellar (late unlamented), IBM.  We have 
    file and compute servers; we also have an Epoch-1 system used for
    file service and backup.  We have no diskless machines (we hate 'em);
    all machines have local storage and local OS.  There are also some
    turnkey systems here, but we don't support those directly.  Also,
    lots of X-terminals -- personally I hate them, but we buy them, so
    the researchers do not get hit with a capital cost (they "lease" them
    back); of course, these things require us to have more server 
    capacity.

    We will support any workstation anybody at this facility buys.  We
    buy the general support machines (servers), but usually the 
    group getting a workstation directly buys it.  (But they all
    belong to Chevron then, anyway, so who cares?)  Same for software:
    software for a single workstation is usually bought by the 
    workstation "owner", but software for general use -- often installed
    on servers of one flavor or the other -- we buy.

    We do not monitor or report usage for Unix machines for charging.
    Of course, we do that for the mainframes, VAX/VMS's, and the Cray.
    We have a nice little simple algorithm for charging.

    We just try to recover costs from our customers, the users of the
    workstations, mainframes, Macs, PCs at our site.  The machines for
    which accounting systems are well-developed and which have well-defined
    and (reasonably) well-understood costs -- the mainframes, VAX/VMS, and
    the Cray -- recover their own costs via the accounting charges,
    mostly for CPU usage, but (on the IBMs and Cray) also sometimes
    for disk usage.  For our Unix workstations and servers, however, with
    so many of them, any such scheme we judge to be non-cost-effective.
    So:  We compute the dollars we must recover yearly, pro-rate by month,
    and then allocate fixed percentages of that into a few categories:
    (1) supported Unix workstations, (2) unsupported Unix workstations,
    which are connected to our IP network, and so still use our services,
    (3) X-terminals, (4) MACs connected to the network, (5) PCs connected
    to our net.  Then the dollar amount in each category is evenly divided
    amoungst all the hosts in that category.  At the end of the
    year, we refund any overcharges to the customers (or they pay
    up any undercharge) so that we *just* recover our costs.

    This is actually a lot simpler in practice than it sounds.  Of course,
    as the number of hosts in each category (except (2)) keeps increasing,
    the monthly charge per host has been decreasing.  The scheme is
    reasonably fair, and is very inexpensive and cheap to implement
    and administer.  And, we asked and received customer acceptance of
    the scheme, and the proportions, before implementing it.  Every
    body seems to be pretty happy with it.

    I can easily imagine situations where this simple a scheme just 
    would be insufficient.  Sometimes computer usage must be charged
    back to external entities, who may be dissatisfied with this
    plan.  I have no good or new ideas for that; but in our situation,
    at our site, our scheme works fine.

Steve Couturie

