 This is a very hard question to answer! One thing I would suggest, if
 you have access to the 'communications of the ACM', I think the december
 '91 issue had an editorial by Rob Kolstad about system administration. I
 think this would be a really good source of ideas about what system
 administrators might have to do at your site, not all of which are 
 obvious beforehand. 
 
 Also, the user population has a lot to do with the staffing needs. 
 Most people think system administrators administer machines, 
 but in fact, many spend more than half their time answering user 
 questions. The level of technical competence of your users is 
 not really the issue here (programmers ask questions too :). 
 I think it has to do with how pressured other workers are. If 
 they have time to read the manual or experiment, they won't ask 
 as many questions; if everyone is under a deadline and short 
 of time, they will shunt even basic RTFM questions to an administrator 
 because they can get the answer faster.

 For the record, the group I'm in now has two geographical sites, 
 50 people, 50 sgi workstations, 30 macs, networks connecting 
 all (including a T1 line), and two system administrator positions.  
 Everyone is on full support for hardware and system software. 
 Applications (sgi animation software, mac word processing etc) 
 are supported through other channels.
 
