From: moss%ibis@cs.umass.edu (Eliot Moss)

Well, we needed a scheme acceptable to federal agencies and auditors, yet
practical to implement and easy to estimate on proposals. Here's what we came
up with: $k per full-time equivalent person PLUS $n per workstation. The
per-FTE charge is derived from the notion of per-person impacts on common
resources such as consulting, mail, printing, etc. The per-ws charge is to
include direct maintenance, network connectivity, s/w maint and upgrades, etc.
Each project determines the fractional FTEs and workstations. Here is an
example:

As a faculty member, the state pays 3/4 of my salary, and hence 3/4 of one FTE
charge and 3/4 of my w/s charge. One of my grants covers 1/4 of my salary (the
summer) and hence 1/4 FTE and 1/4 w/s. Each graduate research assistant I have
counts as 1/2 FTE (20 hours / week), so the grant they are working under is
charge 1/2 FTE plus their workstation.

This works reasonably well, though there are issues regarding MacIntosh
systems which in the past were frequently not networked and not much support
was offered for them. These days, they are networked and more support is
available. Certain specialized equipment is charged directly to the groups
using it. It is clear that certain averaging principles are being applied.
What we'd really prefer is straight per-FTE charges, since they are easier to
determine for proposal budgeting, but the current scheme seems workable. In
our environment the annual charges work out to $1800/w/s and $2000/FTE. This
is higher than many academic institutions (i.e., ones where the state or the
institution subsidizes the environment better) and probably a lot lower than
many business organizations' true cost. Note that these days charges for a PC
should probably be equivalent to charges for a w/s. Servers might be
problematic, and we've yet to figure out about X terminals ....

