Excuse me if I am completely confused, but the SCSI bus of the 3100
will handle 7 devices (of which one is higher priority than the
controller, which may not be a smart idea). So you don't have to idle
the existing RZ23, you can just move it to another SCSI address (or
move the new RZ24 to another address). I have run a 3100-30 with the
local disk set to device 2, 3 and 7, without any problems (just make
sure you boot from the right device, i.e. SET BOOT DKAx00).

As far as I know, no part of VMS cares about the SCSI address at which
devices are found, it just turns the SCSI address into a part of the
device name (SCSI address X turns into DKAX00 or MKAX00, replace "A"
with "B" for the external bus), except X=6 is reserved for the
controllerw. So the choice of device numbers=names for your TZ30 and your
two disks is completely up to you.

Someone please correct me if I am wrong. By the way, little gripe: Why
is the controller hardwired to address 6 ? The 5380 which is used in
the 3100s SCSI controller can be software-programmed for any "Own ID"
address, if my memory is correct (at least its successor the 53C90 can
definitely be software-selected). Is there a particularly good reason
for not allowing the user to move the controller to another address
(with jumpers on the board, or a command in the boot monitor) ?

