First, The original question:

The disk controller for a VAXstation 3100 Model 30 or 38 comes attached to 
what is called the "drive Plate".  It's the thing that holds the disks 
into the system.  (The Model 76 and the VS4000 is different and has the 
SCSI controller on the system motherboard)  Anyway, the drive plate that 
comes with a Model 30 has the SCSI/ST506 controller on it (which is bigger
than the SCSI/SCSI controller that's on the Model 38 controller)  This
leaves less room on the drive plate for disks and things.  When you use the
Model 30 controller (VS42D-HH I think) you thus can only put on either
two disks or one disk and a removeable.  (ie: your TZ30)  When you use the
Model 38 controller, you can put in two disks AND  a removeable.

The Model 38 controller/drive plate is a VS42D-JJ and costs $1000 (list)
and it will fit into the VAXstation 3100 Model 30.  However, you will need
to get new mounting hardware for your older devices since that also changed
beteween Model 30 and Model 38.  (it's just the screws that hold things
togther, in the M30 the drives are screwed in, in the M38, they snap in)

Second, the follow-up question

The controller is set to 6 for the following reason:

	1:	SCSI bus priority is given by the SCSI id, thus a higher 
		number is better.

	2:	However, we wanted to allow one to have a high priority device
		on th bus, so we left "7" open.

On the VAXstation 4000 family, this can be changed with the SET SCSI 
console command.  I don't believe that the 3100 CPU SCSI id can be changed


