In article <1992Feb6.104432.1599@vixvax.mgi.com> cepek@vixvax.mgi.com writes:
>On RT-11, is there a way to find out if a file is protected (without
>having to muddle through the directory itself)?
>
>There don't seem to be any system requests for such a seemingly
>simple task; none of these seem to help:  .CSTAT, .DSTATUS, .ENTER,
>.LOOKUP, .PURGE, .RENAME, etc.
>
>Note that I don't want to _change_ the protection (that's easy with
>.FPROT), I want to find out what it currently is for a file.
>
>In fact, the documentation says that you can do a .LOOKUP on a
>file that is protected and then write to it (honest! look up .FPROT).
>So how the heck am I supposed to know that I _shouldn't_ change
>the file because it is protected?  KEX does it:
>   ?KEX-W-Output file or device protected- Inspect (Y,N) ? _
>
>(At this point, I'd even be willing to entertain something undocumented.)

RT-11 file protection is intended only to ensure that a file is not
deleted accidently.  You can overwrite the file at will.   I don't believe
you can just test the file protection status.  I suspect KED/KEX does
something unethical like look directly at the directory.  You can
get the directory segment from .CSTAT and look yourself w/o searching the
entire directory.   I'm not aware of any other method.

  Of course if you do a .ENTER first it will report failure if protected.
Then you can .Purge I suppose if you really wanted to keep the unprotected
file.  I have not tried this however.





